```

`Cli/CliRunner.cs` stub: `public static class CliRunner { public static int Run(string[] args) { Console.Error.WriteLine("cli not implemented"); return 2; } }`
in namespace `BatchRenameStudio.Cli`.

`Forms/MainForm.cs` stub: `public sealed class MainForm : Form` in namespace
`BatchRenameStudio.Forms`, constructor sets `Text = "Batch Rename Studio Pro";`
and `Size = new Size(1200, 800);`. Nothing else.

## 4. Semantics you must implement literally

These are the customer's words. Where this card adds a ruling it is marked **[R]**.

1. **Scope.** Only regular files directly inside the folder. Non-recursive. Directories
   are excluded entirely. **[R]** Files carrying `FileAttributes.Directory` or
   `ReparsePoint`-to-directory are excluded; hidden/system files ARE included.
2. **Name split.** `dot` = index of the LAST `.` in the file name. If `dot > 0`,
   `base` = name[0..dot) and `ext` = name[dot+1..] (no dot). Otherwise `base` = whole
   name and `ext` = `""`. (`.gitignore` -> base `.gitignore`, ext `""`.)
3. **Target string.** `applyTo:"name"` -> steps operate on `base`.
   `applyTo:"nameAndExtension"` -> steps operate on the whole file name.
4. **Order.** Steps apply in array order, each to the result of the previous.
5. **Processing order.** `sort` ascending. `"name"` = ordinal (codepoint) ascending,
   case-sensitive (`StringComparer.Ordinal` on the file name). `"created"` /
   `"modified"` use filesystem times (`CreationTimeUtc` / `LastWriteTimeUtc`).
   **[R]** Ties in created/modified are broken by ordinal file name, so the order is
   always total and deterministic.
6. **Reassembly.** Proposed name = `base + "." + ext` when `ext` is non-empty, else
   `base`. Under `applyTo:"nameAndExtension"` the target string IS the proposed name;
   an `extension` step there applies to the trailing extension of the current target,
   in place, at its position in the array order.
7. **Status — first match wins**, evaluated per item after every proposal is known:
   * `"invalid"` — proposed name is empty, longer than 255 characters, contains any of
     `< > : " / \ | ? *` or any character below U+0020, or its `base` (rule-2 split of
     the proposed name, case-insensitive) is `CON`, `PRN`, `AUX`, `NUL`, `COM1`-`COM9`,
     `LPT1`-`LPT9`.
   * `"collision"` — equals (case-INsensitive) an existing **file** in the folder other
     than this item, OR equals (case-insensitive) another item's proposed name.
     **[R]** "other than this item" means: ignore the existing file that is this item's
     own original. So a case-only rename (`a.txt` -> `A.txt`) is NOT a collision.
     **[R]** Directories in the folder are not part of the existing-name set.
     **[R]** Items whose own status is invalid still count as "another item" here.
   * `"unchanged"` — equals the original name exactly (ordinal).
   * `"ok"` — otherwise.
8. `items` / `files` order = processing order.
9. Determinism: same inputs -> byte-identical output.
10. `reason` may be any short string or `""`. Fill it usefully (e.g.
    `"contains illegal character '?'"`, `"collides with existing file"`).

### Evaluation algorithm

```
files = scanned, sorted per rule 5
ops   = rules.Steps.Select(OpFactory.Create).ToList()      // built ONCE, reused for every file
for i, file in files:
    st = new EvalState(file.Name, rules.ApplyTo, i)
        Target = (applyTo == Name) ? OriginalBase : OriginalName
        Ext    = OriginalExt
    for k, op in ops:
        before = st.Target
        op.Apply(st)
        record StepTrace { Index = k, Op = rules.Steps[k].Op, Before = before, After = st.Target }
    proposed = st.BuildProposed()
        applyTo == Name              -> NameParts.Join(st.Target, st.Ext)
        applyTo == NameAndExtension  -> st.Target
then assign statuses (rule 7) across the whole list.
```

Note the trace `op` string is the op id from the rules file, even for an unknown op.

## 5. The 14 operations

`OpCatalog.All` order = the order below. Every parameter read goes through `ParamBag`
with the stated default, so a missing JSON property never throws.

1. **`replace`** — `find`(text,""), `replaceWith`(text,""), `regex`(bool,false), `ignoreCase`(bool,false).
   Replaces ALL occurrences. `regex:true` uses .NET `Regex`; `replaceWith` may use `$1`
   group references. **Empty `find` is a no-op (never throw).**
   **[R]** A `Regex` that fails to compile, or that hits the match timeout, leaves the
   target unchanged (no-op). Construct every `Regex` in this project with
   `RegexOptions.None` (+ `IgnoreCase` when asked) and a `TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1)` timeout.
   Non-regex mode uses `string.Replace(find, replaceWith, ignoreCase ? OrdinalIgnoreCase : Ordinal)`.
2. **`insert`** — `text`(text,""), `position`(choice: prefix|suffix|index, "prefix"), `index`(int,0).
   `index` is 0-based, clamped to `[0, length]`.
3. **`remove`** — `from`(int,0), `count`(int,0). `from` clamped to `[0, length]`;
   `count` clamped to what remains (and to `>= 0`).
4. **`sequence`** — `pattern`(text,"{n:000}_"), `start`(int,1), `step`(int,1),
   `position`(choice: prefix|suffix, "prefix").
   `pattern` may contain literal text plus exactly one `{n:PAD}` token, `PAD` being one
   or more `0` giving the zero-pad width. Each sequence step uses its OWN start/step:
   the counter is `start + i * step` where `i` is the file's 0-based processing index.
   `start` is always >= 0. **[R]** Compute the counter as `long` to avoid overflow;
   render with `counter.ToString(new string('0', padWidth), CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)`.
   **[R]** Only the FIRST `{n:PAD}` token is substituted; further ones stay literal.
   **[R]** A pattern with no token is used literally.
   `prefix` -> rendered + target, `suffix` -> target + rendered.
5. **`case`** — `mode`(choice: upper|lower|title, "upper").
   `title` = for each maximal run of Unicode letters (`char.IsLetter`), uppercase the
   first character and lowercase the rest; non-letters untouched. Invariant culture.
6. **`extension`** — `mode`(choice: lower|upper|set, "lower"), `value`(text,"txt").
   * Under `applyTo:"name"`: transforms `state.Ext`, `state.Target` is untouched
     (so `before` == `after` in the trace). `lower`/`upper` on an empty ext = no change.
     `set` -> `Ext = value` (value carries no leading dot; **[R]** if the user typed a
     leading dot, strip exactly one).
   * Under `applyTo:"nameAndExtension"`: split the CURRENT target with rule 2 into
     `(b, e)`; `lower`/`upper` -> if `e` is empty, no change, else
     `Target = NameParts.Join(b, transform(e))`; `set` -> `Target = NameParts.Join(b, value)`
     (so a file with no extension gains one, and an empty `value` removes it).
7. **`pad`** — `length`(int,10), `fill`(text,"_"), `side`(choice: left|right, "left").
   If the target is shorter than `length`, add copies of `fill` on `side` until it
   reaches exactly `length`. No change if the target is already that long, or if `fill`
   is not exactly one character (`fill.Length != 1`). **[R]** `length` is clamped to
   `[0, 4096]` as an allocation guard.
8. **`trim`** — `chars`(text," _-"), `side`(choice: both|left|right, "both").
   Repeatedly remove characters from the given side(s) while they appear in the `chars`
   set. Empty `chars` is a no-op.
9. **`slug`** — `separator`(text,"-").
   Lowercase the target (invariant), replace every maximal run of characters outside
   `[a-z0-9]` with a single `separator`, then remove leading and trailing separators.
   An empty `separator` removes those runs entirely. **[R]** "remove leading and
   trailing separators" = while the result starts with the separator string, cut it;
   same at the end (ordinal, only when the separator is non-empty).
10. **`translate`** — `from`(text,""), `to`(text,"").
    Character by character: the character at index `k` of `from` becomes the character
    at index `k` of `to`. If `to` is shorter than `from`, characters mapped beyond its
    end are DELETED. If `from` repeats a character, the FIRST mapping wins. Characters
    not in `from` are untouched.
11. **`numberFormat`** — `width`(int,4).
    Find the FIRST maximal run of `[0-9]` and left-pad it with `0` to `width`. No change
    if there is no digit run, or the run is already `>= width`. **[R]** `width` clamped
    to `[0, 4096]`.
12. **`extract`** — `pattern`(text,"\\d+"), `group`(int,0).
    Replace the whole target with the first regex match. `group` 0 is the whole match.
    No match, or a group index out of range, yields an EMPTY target.
    **[R]** A pattern that fails to compile, or a match timeout, is a no-op (target
    unchanged) — that is an author error, not a "no match".
    **[R]** A valid but unmatched group (e.g. an optional group) yields an empty target.
13. **`template`** — `format`(text,"{name}-{ext}-{n:000}").
    Replace the whole target with `format`, substituting `{name}` = the current target,
    `{ext}` = the ORIGINAL file's extension (rule 2), `{len}` = the current target's
    length in decimal, `{n:PAD}` = the file's 0-based processing index PLUS ONE,
    zero-padded to `PAD` width. Any other `{...}` sequence is left in place literally.
    **[R]** "current target" = the target as it was when this step started; take one
    snapshot and use it for every `{name}` / `{len}` occurrence.
    **[R]** Token matching is ordinal and case-sensitive: `{name}`, `{ext}`, `{len}`,
    and `{n:` followed by one-or-more `0` then `}`. `{n}` alone stays literal.
    **[R]** Scan with a regex over `\{[^{}]*\}`; unmatched braces stay literal.
14. **`dedupeChars`** — no parameters. Collapse every run of the same character to one
    occurrence. Case-sensitive.

## 6. `rules.json` parsing

```json
{ "applyTo": "name" | "nameAndExtension",
  "sort": "name" | "created" | "modified",
  "steps": [ { "op": "...", ...op params... } ] }
```

* `applyTo` / `sort` matched case-insensitively; missing or unrecognised -> the default
  (`Name` / `Name`). **[R]** unrecognised values do not throw.
* `steps` missing -> empty list. A step element that is not an object, or has no string
  `op`, is **skipped**. **[R]**
* Every other property of a step object goes into the `ParamBag`:
  string -> `string`, true/false -> `bool`, number -> `int` (truncate a fractional
  number toward zero; out-of-range -> clamp to `int` range), null -> skip,
  array/object -> skip. **[R]**
* Malformed JSON (`JsonException`) -> `RuleParseException`.
* `ToJson()` writes `applyTo`, `sort`, `steps`, and inside each step `op` first then the
  params in insertion order; indented; round-trips through `FromJson`.

## 7. Acceptance for this card

Run these yourself before reporting done:

1. `cd /d D:\BenchRuns\XL-B-03 && dotnet build -c Release` -> `Build succeeded`,
   `0 Warning(s)`, `0 Error(s)`. Paste the tail of the output in your report.
2. Confirm the produced binary exists at
   `src\BatchRenameStudio\bin\Release\net8.0-windows\BatchRenameStudio.exe`.
3. Do not run the GUI; do not attempt any rename.

Report back: the file list you created, the build output tail, and any place where you
had to make a judgement call not covered above.

